                        "tags": [
                            "comparative"
                        ],
                        "form": "more Pelasgian"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "346b933b-6a97-45a3-87a2-a7cf5a86089b",
                        "entry_id": "9f51aa65-6a91-3dc5-a0ce-bd9b1ccc2624",
                        "tags": [
                            "superlative"
                        ],
                        "form": "most Pelasgian"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9cf4c6ba-470d-3454-a6bc-00d6ae01dd1d",
                        "entry_id": "9f51aa65-6a91-3dc5-a0ce-bd9b1ccc2624",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of or pertaining to the Pelasgians, their culture, etc."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "79ab4508-45d2-41f9-98ac-a4de5c21c25b",
                                "sense_id": "9cf4c6ba-470d-3454-a6bc-00d6ae01dd1d",
                                "text": "The very solemn and ancient observance of her^([Demeter's]) worship in Attica, which was so eminently a Pelasgian state in the time of Homer, entirely accords with the indications of the Homeric text."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c9fc0a75-1862-4d11-be27-06b24d7bd6ca",
                                "sense_id": "9cf4c6ba-470d-3454-a6bc-00d6ae01dd1d",
                                "text": "Croesus learns that of the Spartans and the Athenians, the one ethnos, the Spartans, was Dorian, the other, the Athenians, was Ionian; the one (obviously Athenians) were Pelasgian of old, the other (the Dorians) were Greek (Hellenikon)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1080cdd5-5baa-4e01-bbd6-8fa1c80489e4",
                                "sense_id": "9cf4c6ba-470d-3454-a6bc-00d6ae01dd1d",
                                "text": "Until recently, the investigation of Pelasgian language and culture was treated as a special field of Indo-European studies because the majority of scholars were convinced that the pre–Greek population of Greece must have been of Indo-European ethnic stock."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
